I feel like the essence of what made the original

00:00:27.009 --> 00:00:31.699
so interesting is the thing that's not really
focused on.

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:38.770
Usually what's focused on in a sequel is more
and it's more spectacle not the thing that

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:44.050
made it compelling to begin with I think the
way to answer this question is to look at

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:52.359
the sequels that that you know succeeded,
films like The Godfather II right?

00:00:52.359 --> 00:01:00.390
Godfather II you know was an even more complex
film than the original Godfather.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:06.680
It tells it it plays with time where you're
having two stories being told simultaneously

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.960
the story of Vito Corleone his rise to power
you know along with you know the pre the present-day

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:19.160
Michael Corleone who's you know you're seeing
his story play out in present day that the

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:25.250
story is being told and it's also different
and it's original in the same way that the

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:30.600
first godfather was original I could apply
that same thing to star wars and the empire

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:36.600
strikes back the empire strikes back ended
on a cliffhanger the original star wars ended

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.960
with this triumphant ending the good guys
win when did the good guys ever lose in a

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:48.850
fairy tale generally never but the empire
strikes back did that it didn't even tell

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:54.850
a complete story you know and at the time
you had to wait three years to to see what

00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:01.210
the conclusion of that story would be so doing
something original and different rather than

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:06.170
more of the same which would have been let's
have another death star which of course they

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:12.970
did twice more in star wars films you know
I mean maybe three times if you want to count

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:20.690
rogue one but going over old territory is
boring and I feel like what you need to do

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:26.170
is in the same way that the original film
was a risk in doing something original if

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:32.770
you're going to do a sequel you also need
to take risks and do something different I

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:40.140
would I would say that this doesn't apply
as much but with alien and aliens alien directed

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:47.260
by Ridley Scott Aliens James Cameron one's
more horror set in outer space the other one

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:56.800
is action movie more aliens right but it's
a different type of film and a continuation

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:04.861
of that story told with with some originality
that's another sequel that holds up but so

00:03:04.861 --> 00:03:11.510
many other sequels that I have not named are
terrible for the very reason that they just

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:19.010
try to give you more of what was in the first
one rather than you know defy your expectations

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:26.530
and and that I think has to do with fear on
the part of the filmmakers and not really

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:32.080
trusting the audience I think you need to
trust the audience that they want to see these

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.540
beloved characters from the first film in
new and different situations things that are

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:45.850
challenging to those characters and and you
don't often see that in sequels it's just

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:53.900
more I think that's lazy as much as the fast
and the furious movies are financially successful

